{
  "about": "Each Chrome API and web feature the first-party sources use, with the first Chrome version that has it and the page that gives it: Chrome's extension API reference, or the compatibility data on the MDN page named (#300, from #27's Toolchain and AC10). Before it bundles, npm run build scans the scripts under js/ but js/lib/, which is vendored, and the stylesheets under css/: for each chrome.* member, read as chrome, its namespace and the member read in it, so chrome.storage.local.get reads as chrome.storage.local and an event's addListener as the event; for each web global scripts/build/js-scan.js lists; and for each CSS at-rule scripts/build/chrome-features.js lists. A feature with no row fails the build as chrome/feature, and so does chrome read whole, since it hides the members it reaches. A namespace read into a variable counts as the namespace. A row above manifest.json's minimum_chrome_version fails it as chrome/version, and the bundler's target is the highest row. Where MDN gives a version as ≤N, the row takes N. test/build-chrome-features.test.js holds the rows to exactly what the scan finds.",
